<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chuvash people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chuvash are a Turkic people of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, most of whom live in the Chuvash Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with its capital at Cheboksary. Their language is the only living member of the Oghur branch of Turkic, and they are widely seen as descendants of the Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. Unlike their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> neighbours, most were converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> under Russian rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chuvash-people.jpg" alt="Chuvash people" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zelenov Ivan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Chuvash_men.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>There is no universally accepted etymology of the name Chuvash. One theory derives it from Common Turkic jăvaş, 'friendly' or 'peaceful'; another from Tabghach, the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> clan that founded the Northern Wei dynasty, which Gerard Clauson showed could become Chuvash through regular sound changes. Two schools place the people's origin differently: a mixing of the Sabir tribes with Finno-Ugrians, or descent from the Volga Bulgars. After Old Bulgaria broke up in the second half of the 7th century, the Sabirs and other Oghuric tribes migrated to the Volga-Kama region and founded Volga Bulgaria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed Volga Bulgaria in 1236, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and then the Kazan Khanate ruled the Chuvash. The modern name appears in records from the sixteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> conquered the Kazan Khanate in 1552; the Chuvash had to pay yasak and gradually lost much of their land, and many farmers became bonded laborers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> industry or worked on barges. Most were not converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> until the mid-19th century.</p><p>A written script was created in 1871. The Chuvash Autonomous Region was established on 24 June 1920 and became a Soviet republic on 21 April 1925. Soviet authorities redrew its borders, leaving many Chuvash in neighbouring republics, and the language vanished from education and public use for most of the Soviet period. A cultural revival began in 1989.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Chuvash is the only surviving member of the Oghur branch of Turkic, marked by r for Common Turkic z and l for š. It was first thought a Turkified Finno-Ugric language until August Ahlqvist's fieldwork of 1856. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> considers it endangered. Most Chuvash are Eastern Orthodox Christians; a minority are Sunni Muslims or follow Vattisen Yaly, a revival of the ethnic religion. Some pre-Christian traditions survive, including prayer and goose sacrifice at shrines called keremet, and distinctive embroidery on traditional clothing. A 2017 genome study found a largely Finno-Ugric genetic component beside a Turkic one shared with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-tatars">Volga Tatars</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chuvash people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Chuvash</b>, spoken today in the Volga region of Russia, is the only living language of the Oghuric branch of Turkic, the branch spoken by the Volga Bulgars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In late 1917 the National Assembly of Muslims of Inner Russia and Siberia resolved to create the Idel-Ural State, uniting Tatars, Bashkirs and <b>Chuvash</b>, with Kazan as capital, and its proclamation was planned for March 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/idel-ural-state">Idel-Ural State</a></li></ul></section>
